# Φ x, each part put into the composed layer's unit. One row per resolved part and quantity it
# states, carrying that figure already multiplied by the part's factor; `F` is the grouping that
# composition/fused.sqlc applies afterwards.
#
# The conversion is diagonal
#   That is why it multiplies instead of mixing. Each part converts by its own single factor;
#   nothing here combines two parts' units. That is what lets the sum downstream be a sum at all.
#
# The sign picks the corner, and one rule reads both signs
#   A factor is strictly positive, so it can never reorder a claim, but it does decide which corner
#   each bound takes. Where the figure is non-negative the low takes the factor's low and the high
#   takes its high, which is the plain bound-by-bound product. Where the figure is negative that
#   reverses, because more of a shortfall converted at a larger rate is the smaller number. Taking
#   the least and the greatest over both corners is one rule that reads both cases, so this relation
#   and composition/settled_remainders.sqlc convert by the same rule rather than by two.
#
#   ⛔ Bound by bound alone would be correct only while every figure here is non-negative, and
#   nothing establishes that: the three points are `xs:decimal` with no lower bound and no rule is
#   about a claim's sign. A margin ratio is a subject this model admits and a loss-making one is
#   negative, so the figure that would break the plain product is a filing the model should accept,
#   not one it should refuse. On this corpus the two forms agree everywhere, and
#   composition/conversion_slopes.sqlc is where which corner each filing took can be read.
#
# A missing factor means one, exactly
#   An absent factor means one, exactly, and never "unknown". A part with no factor is a part whose
#   units already agree. A part whose factor is a typed absence inside the claim is a different
#   document (somebody who says nobody measured the conversion), and `coalesce` reads only the
#   first. Confusing them would silently assert a conversion no filer made.
#
# The factor is correlated with itself
#   One factor multiplies both a part's nameplate and its demand, so the two converted intervals
#   move together. Difference them as though they were independent and the spread gets counted
#   twice, which is why a composed remainder is converted and never re-derived. See
#   queries/matrices/4-converted-remainder.sql.
#
# It is Φ applied to every quantity a fusion sums
#   One row per part and quantity, from layers/summed_quantities.sqlc: demand, nameplate and draw,
#   each converted by the same factor, and the `quantity` column says which. A part that leaves a
#   quantity unstated has no row for it here, and composition/unstated_quantities.sqlc suspends
#   that quantity's sum rather than letting the part drop out of it.
#
# A derived part is read computed
#   The figures come from composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc, so a part filing its quantity
#   `derived` enters at the figure its own fusions compute, and a part whose computation cannot
#   finish has no row, exactly like a part that leaves the quantity unstated.
#
# A typed-absent factor is not a factor of one
#   `coalesce(factor, 1)` is only sound for an omitted element, which is why the last condition of
#   the join excludes the typed absence. The two are different filings and the schema gives them
#   different words. No `asrt:factor` at all means the part's unit and the composed layer's
#   already agree, so the conversion is the identity and nothing is being assumed. A factor filed
#   as `unmeasured` means a composer looked at a real unit crossing and could not size it;
#   converting that by one asserts the units agree, which is the claim they declined to make.
#   `every-unsized-conversion/shift-capacity` is that filing, and the fixture is named for it.
#
# Dropping a part is honest only where the sum is not owed
#   Dropping a part from a sum is only honest if the sum is not owed, so the same condition is a
#   suspension ground in `composition/unsized_conversions.sqlc`. Read the two together: one
#   removes the part, the other removes the obligation.
#
# The identity case is checked, not trusted
#   `checks/unit_crossing_without_a_factor` fires where the units differ and no factor is filed,
#   so an omitted element can only ever mean the units already agree.
-- asrt:Part/asrt:factor applied to each quantity the part layer states.
SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer, p.part_filing, p.part_layer, s.quantity,
       least(   s.low  * coalesce(p.factor_low, 1), s.low  * coalesce(p.factor_high, 1)) AS low,
       s.mode * coalesce(p.factor_mode, 1)                                               AS mode,
       greatest(s.high * coalesce(p.factor_low, 1), s.high * coalesce(p.factor_high, 1)) AS high
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc)
) s
       ON s.filing = p.part_filing AND s.layer = p.part_layer
  AND s.low IS NOT NULL
  AND p.factor_state IN ('omitted', 'stated')
